          #BirkhoffHSP.num_example
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Birkhoff’s HSP theorem)
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/language"} language
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              generated by a set of (single-sorted) finitary operations, and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+in+model+theory"} structures
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is the class of models for a set of universally quantified equations between terms of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              (a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  (H) The class is closed under homomorphic
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/images"} images
                  (see
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %a{:href => "#HomomorphicImages"} below
              %li
                %p (S) The class is closed under subalgebras,
              %li
                %p (P) The class is closed under taking products.
          %p In a more classical wording/terminology it is phrased as
          %p (Birkhoff’s variety theorem) A class of algebras of the same signature is a variety of algebras iff it is closed under homomorphic images, subalgebras and arbitrary (small) products.
          %p
            See also at
            %em
              %a{:href => "Lawvere+theory#CharacterizationOfExamples"} Lawvere theory – Characterization of examples
          #HomomorphicImages.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Here “closed under homomorphic images” means that if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a structure in the class and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              is a structure for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon A \to B
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              between them, then also its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi im
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(\phi) \hookrightarrow B
              is an element of the class.
          #FirstOrderAnalogue.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+logic"} first-order
              analogue of HSP (theorem
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BirkhoffHSP"}
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#ChangKeisler66"} Chang-Keisler 66
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+classes+of+structures"} elementary classes of structures
              of structures: they’re precisely those closed under
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+substructures"} elementary substructures
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+embeddings"} elementary embeddings
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraproducts"} ultraproducts
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraroots"} ultraroots
              (if an ultrapower of something is in your class, that something was in your class.)
          %h2#references References
          %p The original reference is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Garrett+Birkhoff"}> Garrett Birkhoff
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the structure of abstract algebras
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0305004100013463"} doi
              %a{:href => "https://www.math.hawaii.edu/~ralph/Classes/619/birkhoff1935.pdf"} pdf
          %p There is a textbook treatment in the monograph
          %ul
            %li#ChangKeisler66
              %p
                Chang, Keisler
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Continuous Model Theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"}> Michael Barr
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em HSP type theorems in the category of posets
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Proc. 7th International Conf. Mathematical Foundation of Programming Language Semantics
                %strong 598
                (1992) 221–234 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-55511-0_11"}> doi:10.1007/3-540-55511-0_11
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Barr-HSPinPosets.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"}> Michael Barr
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Functorial semantics and HSP type theorems
                %strong 31
                (1994) 223–251 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01236519"}> doi:10.1007/BF01236519
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.mcgill.ca/barr/papers/hsp.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Barr-FuncSemanticsHSP.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"}> Michael Barr
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em HSP subcategories of Eilenberg-Moore algebras
                %strong 10
                18 (2002), 461–468 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/10/18/10-18abs.html"}> tac:10-18
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Goldblatt"}> Robert Goldblatt
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em What is the coalgebraic analogue of Birkhoff’s variety theorem?
                = succeed ":1\u20132" do
                  %strong 266
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0304-3975(00)00395-9"} doi
          %p Some Birkhoff’s variety-style theorems in a categorical setup for equational logic play role in
          %ul
            %li
              G. Roşu,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Complete categorical deduction for satisfaction as injectivity
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 4060
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/11780274_9"} doi
            %li
              G. Roşu,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Axiomatizability in inclusive equational logics
              = succeed ":5" do
                %strong 12
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129501003474"} doi
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Variety_(universal_algebra)#Birkhoff's_variety_theorem"} Birkhoff's theorem
          .property
            category:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/algebra"} algebra
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/logic"} logic
